Matt Pocock puts dependency injection first:

> Now, what I think people get wrong is that they think that error handling and the type-safe nature of Effect is the reason you go for Effect. I think the reason you go for Effect is dependency injection, actually. It allows you to create these beautiful small modules that you can just swap out as needed and create these really nice looking tests with Effect too.

Dillon Mulroy compares Effect with Spring and NestJS:

> Effect has probably the most novel dependency injection system that I've worked with. I've worked in enterprise languages like Java, done the whole Spring system. I've worked with NestJS. Effect's dependency injection is genuinely fantastic and really, really nice to work with.

