## Requirements stay visible

An `Effect<A, E, R>` describes its result, failure, and required services. The `R` type tracks those requirements. The compiler rejects execution while a required service remains in `R`.

A service gives callers a typed interface. A Layer builds its implementation and declares its construction requirements. Shared Layer identity reuses construction within the same memoization context.

## A service captures its dependencies

`FileInspector.make` obtains `FileSystem` during construction. Its `inspect` method uses that captured service. The method's type carries no filesystem requirement.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/file-inspector.ts at=c39e106e33eaa00432454ed31c59fa90389255d0 lines=6-16 title="FileInspector captures FileSystem"

```

The static Layer builds the service through `make`.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/file-inspector.ts at=c39e106e33eaa00432454ed31c59fa90389255d0 lines=17-35 title="The inspection method and static Layer"

```

## Tests supply another implementation

The inspection model test supplies a controlled `FileInspector`. Its fake waits for a result chosen by the test. One `provideService` replaces the implementation.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/test/inspect-machine.model.test.ts at=c39e106e33eaa00432454ed31c59fa90389255d0 lines=125-135 title="A controlled FileInspector"

```

`Effect.provide` supplies a Layer or Context. `Effect.provideService` supplies one service directly. Both remove the supplied service from the requirements type.

